.Dd September 28, 2026
.Dt BOOTLOG 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm bootlog ,
.Nm screenlog ,
.Nm framefail
.Nd kernel log, screen log level, and frame allocation test device
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Pa /dev/bootlog
.Pp
.Pa /dev/screenlog
.Pp
.Pa /dev/framefail
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Ss Kernel log
.Pa /dev/bootlog
reads the retained kernel records from the current boot.
.Pp
The offset counts from the oldest retained byte.
Records may be evicted between chunk copies, including within one read.
The returned bytes are not a snapshot.
.Pp
The device seeks to any place through 4294967295, the last its offset
has, and
.Dv SEEK_END
seeks from the end of the retained bytes; the older 32-bit
.Xr lseek 2
returns no place past 2147483647 and refuses one with
.Er EOVERFLOW .
A read holds the open file description for its length, as a seek of it
does, so a seek through a descriptor that shares the description waits
for a read in progress and moves the offset on from where the read left
it, as Linux holds a file's position lock.
The
.Xr dmesg 1
script reads this device.
.Ss Screen log level
.Pa /dev/screenlog
controls which kernel records appear on the screen.
A read returns the level as one digit followed by a newline, and a
read at the end of those two bytes returns zero.
A read holds the open file description for its length, so a second read
through a descriptor that shares it waits and goes on from where the
first left the offset.
The device does not seek:
.Xr lseek 2
fails with
.Er ESPIPE .
A write takes its first digit as the new level and accepts the whole write.
An empty write returns zero; a first byte other than a decimal digit
fails with
.Er EINVAL .
.Pp
Levels are clamped to 2 through 7.
The screen shows records at or below the selected number:
.Bl -tag -width 6 -compact
.It 2
Critical errors.
.It 3
Errors.
.It 4
Warnings.
.It 6
Information.
.It 7
Debug messages.
.El
.Pp
Critical errors cannot be hidden.
The setting controls kernel screen output while init owns the console.
.Ss Frame allocation test
.Pa /dev/framefail
is a test device for allocation-failure probes.
A write beginning with a decimal count arms a failure on that numbered
frame allocation during a system call.
The failure occurs once; a count of zero disarms it.
Bytes after the initial decimal digits are ignored, and a successful
write returns its full byte count.
A write without an initial digit fails with
.Er EINVAL .
Arming requires
.Ql rach2.framefail
on the kernel command line; otherwise the write fails with
.Er EPERM .
Reads return end of file.
.Sh FILES
All three nodes are character devices owned by root.
.Bl -tag -width /dev/screenlog
.It Pa /dev/bootlog
Major 240, minor 2; mode 0400.
.It Pa /dev/screenlog
Major 240, minor 38; mode 0600.
.It Pa /dev/framefail
Major 240, minor 37; mode 0600.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr dmesg 1 ,
.Xr console 4 ,
.Xr proc 5
